    "ls|LS16_S9_U_21": " \nGovernment of India\n \nMinistry of Culture\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 21\n \nTO BE ANSWERED \nON \n1\n8\n.\n0\n7\n.2016\n \n \n \nGRANT FOR MULTI\n-\nPURPOSE CULTURAL COMPLEXES\n \n \n \n21. \nSHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY\n:\n \nWill the \nMINISTER OF \nCULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Governmen\nt has stopped releasing funds to the proposals received for \nrelease of grants for construction of Multi\n-\nPurpose Cultural Complexes;\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nwhether already sanctioned MPCCs for which grants were partly released have also \nbeen include\nd in the list and if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe details of the cases pending for release of grants for MPCC Scheme; and\n \n \n(e)\n \nthe steps being taken by the Government to settle the pending MPCC cases at the \nearliest?\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE \n(\nINDEPENDENT \nC\nHARGE\n) \nF\nOR CULTURE \n&\n \nTOURISM\n \n \n(\nDR.\n \nMAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n(a)\n \nNo\n \nMadam.\n \n(b) \n      \n \nDoes not arise.\n \n(c\n)\n \nDoes not arise.\n \n(d)\n \nNo cases are pending on behalf of this Ministry for release of grant under MPCC \nscheme.\n \n(e)\n \nThe Ministry has reminded repeatedly the project aut\nhorities to submit the requisite \ndocuments/certificates/reports etc. to settle the pending MPCC cases.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_210": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 210 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016 \n \nRegional Centres of ICHR \n \n210. DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURC E DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the regional centre s of the Indian Council of \nHistorical Research (ICHR) in  Bengaluru and Guwahati have provided local scholars with \nthe necessary infrastructure and resources to conduct research, thus promoting regional \nhistorical and cultural research; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Ministry would consider replicating this model by providing financial \nassistance to State Governments including t he State Government of Kerala to open new \nregional centres of the ICHR to promote local historical and cultural research; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) Yes Madam. Regional Cent res of the Indian Council of  Historical Research (ICHR) \nat Bengaluru and Guwahati maintains specialized li braries of historical records to assist \nthe Post-Graduate students & Res earch scholars in history, Indology & Culture. Among \nother activities, the centres conduct seminars/ workshops, etc. to disseminate historical \nknowledge in their respective regions and also undertake Research Projects. \n \n(b) to (d) Regional Centre s of ICHR are directly fu nded by ICHR and no financial \nassistance is given to State Governments.  There is no proposal to set up any new \nRegional Centre of ICHR in any State including the state of Kerala. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_211": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 211 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2016 \n \nPROFIT TO OMC \n  \n211. SHRI: B.V. NAIK:  \nSHRI KANWAR SINGH TANWAR:  \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkÑfrd xSl ea=h \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is an increase in profit of public sector oil companies after implementation of market \nlinked pricing mechanism and if so, the details thereof along with profit earned b y them during the \nlast three years, company-wise;  \n \n(b) the total volume of petrol and diesel being supplied by these companies and its percentage of the \ntotal supply in the country;  \n \n(c) whether the prices of petroleum products have been brought down by the Government recently and \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) the extent to which petroleum companies have recovered their losses in view of the reduction in \ncrude oil prices in the international m arket and the current status of the losses being suffered by them \ndespite the subsidy being given to them by the Government during the said period; and  \n \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government/public sector oil companies to minimise these \nlosses? \n \nANSWER \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkÑfrd xSl ea=h (Lora= izHkkj) \n¼Jh /keZsUnz iz/kku½ \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)   \n(a): the Profit after Tax (PAT) of Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) during \nthe last three years is given below: \n  (Rs.crore) \n 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nIOCL 7019 5273 10399 \nBPCL 4061 5085 7432 \nHPCL 1734 2733 3863 \n \n \n\n \n(b):     The details of Petrol and Diesel consumption by Public Sector OMCs along with their \npercentage to total consumption are given below: \n�\nYear \nMS HSD \nPublic \nSector \nOMC \nSales (in \nTMT) \nTotal \nConsum\nption (in \nTMT) \n% of total \nconsumpti\non \nPublic \nSector \nOMC Sales \n(in TMT) \nTotal \nConsumption \n(in TMT) \n% of total \nconsumption \n2013-14 16726 17128 97.7 68166 68364 99.7 \n2014-15 18587 19075 97.4 68661 69416 98.9 \n2015-16 21077 21846 96.5 71977 74639 96.4 \n (c): OMCs have reduced the retail selling price of Petrol and Diesel by Rs. 0.89/litre and \nRs.0.49/litre respectively at Delhi with effect from 1 st July, 2016 and made further reduction \nof Rs. 2.25/litre and Rs.0.42/litre in retail selling price of Petrol and Diesel respectively at \nDelhi with effect from 16th July, 2016. \n(d) & (e): All petroleum products except PDS Kerosene and Subsidized Domestic LPG \nhave been deregulated and the Public Sector OMCs decide their retail prices in line with their \ninternational product prices and other market conditions. As regards PDS Kerosene and \nSubsidized domestic LPG, the subsidy burden on account of sale below market price in being \nborne by the Government and Public Sector upstream oil companies.  \nAs stated in reply to part (a) above, the profit of the Public Sector OMCs have \nincreased in 2015 -16 as compared to the immediately preceding two years i.e. 2013 -14 and \n2014-15.  \nx-x-x-x-x�",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_212": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 212\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016\n \n \nN\nEW POLICY FOR MSME SECTOR\n \n \n212.\n \nSHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n(a) the details of the sale of products of Khadi & Village industries during each of the last \nthree years;\n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to frame a new policy for the development of the \nMicro, Small and Medium\n \nEnterprises (MSMEs) sector in the country; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the time by which it is likely to be implemented? \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \n  \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH)\n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n \n \nThe details of the sale of products of Khadi & Village Industries during \neach of the last three years is given below:                                                      \n \n(Rs. in crore)\n \nSales\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16*\n \nKhadi\n \n1081.04\n \n1170.38\n \n1383.22\n \nVillage \nIndustries\n \n30073.16\n \n31965.52\n \n39680.95\n \n         \nTOTAL\n \n31154.20\n \n33135.90\n \n41064.17\n \n*provisional \n \n \n(b\n)&\n(c)\n: \n \n \nGovernment has constituted a One\n-\nMan Committee headed by Shri \nPrabhat Kumar for framing a policy for development of MSME’s in the country.\n \n \n* * *\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_213": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 213 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.7.2016 \n \nPROTECTION OF ENDANGERED LANGUAGES \n \n213.  SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \na) whether the Government has noticed that a large number of languages have gone extinct in \nthe country since the time of independence, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefore; \nb) whether United Nations Educational Scientific  and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO) has \nfound there are 197 endangered languages in the country with 42 classified as critically endangered \nwhich are included in the list Niha li, traced to the pre-Aryan and pre- munda period, if so, the details \nthereof; and  \nc) the steps taken by the Government to protect all languages in the country? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI. PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam . The Government is aware that a few languages have become extinct since \nindependence. These languages became extinct because either the speakers of these languages died or \nthere are no first language speakers left. \n(b) The information of 197 endangered languages and 42 critically endangered languages of that \nlist, as classified by UNESCO, is at annexure. \n(c) The Government of India has initiated a Sche me known as “Protection and Preservation of \nEndangered Languages of India” (SPPEL). Under th is Scheme, the Central Institute of Indian \nLanguages (CIIL), Mysore works on protection, pr eservation and documentation of all the mother \ntongues/languages of India spoken by less than 10, 000 people.  The Government has also initiated a \nProject known as Bharatavani implemented by the CIIL for all languages, especially the languages \nspoken by more than ten thousand persons, in the cy berspace. This Project aims to build a searchable \nknowledge repository in and about all the languages in India in multimedia (text, audio, video, \nimages) formats through an online portal. \n \n*******     \n  \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO REPLY TO PART (b) of LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 213 ASKED BY SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA FOR \n18.07.2016 REGARDING PROTECTION OF ENDANGERED LANGUAGES \n \n1 A'tong 41  Darma 81 Kodagu \n2 Adi 42  Deori 82 Koireng \n3 Ahom 43  Dimasa 83 Kokborok \n4 Aimol 44  Gadaba 84 Kolami \n5 Aiton 45  Galo 85 Kom \n6 Aka 46  Gangte 86 Konda \n7 Aka 47  Garhwali 87 Konyak \n8 Anal 48  Geta? 88 Koraga \n9 Andro 49  Gondi 89 Korku \n10 Angami 50  Gorum 90 Koro \n11 Angika 51  Great Andamanese 91 Korwa \n12 Ao 52  Gutob 92 Kota \n13 Apatani 53  Handuri 93 Kui \n14 Asur 54  Hill Miri 94 Kului \n15 Badaga 55  Hmar 95 Kumaoni \n16 Baghati 56  Ho 96 Kundal Shahi \n17 Balti 57  Hrangkhol 97 Kurru \n18 Bangani 58  Idu 98 Kuruba \n19 Bangni 59  Irula 99 Kurux \n20 Bawm 60  Jad 100 Kuvi \n21 Bellari 61  Jangshung 101 Ladakhi \n22 Bhadravahi 62  Jarawa 102 Lamgang \n23 Bhalesi 63  Jaunsari 103 Lamongse \n24 Bharmauri 64  Juang 104 Langrong \n25 Bhumji 65  Kabui 105 Lepcha \n26 Biete 66  Kachari 106 Lhota \n27 Birhor 67  Kanashi 107 Liangmai \n28 Bishnupriya Manipuri Creole 68  Kangdi 108 Limbu \n29 Bodo 69  Karbi 109 Lishpa \n30 Bokar 70  Khamba 110 Luro \n31 Bori 71  Khampti 111 Mahasui \n32 Brokshat 72  Kharia 112 Malto \n33 Bunan 73  Khasali 113 Manchad \n34 Byangsi 74  Kheza 114 Manda \n35 Chambeali 75  Khiamngan 115 Mandeali \n36 Chang 76  Khoirao 116 Mao \n37 Chokri 77  Khowa 117 Mara \n38 Churahi 78  Kinnauri 118 Maram \n39 Cuona Menba 79  Koch 119 Maring \n40 Dakpa  80  Koda 120 Mech \n \n \n\n121 Meithei 161 Sangtam \n122 Miji 162 Sengmai \n123 Miju 163 Sentilese \n124 Milang 164 Sherdukpen \n125 Minyong 165 Sherpa \n126 Mising 166 Shompen \n127 Mizo 167 Simi \n128 Motuo Menba 168 Singpho \n129 Moyon 169 Sirmaudi \n130 Mra 170 Sora \n131 Mundari 171 Spiti \n132 Muot 172 Sulung \n133 Mzieme 173 Tagin