    "ls|LS16_S9_U_243": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *243\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.08.2016\n \n \n“FUNDS UNDER PMKVY”\n \n \n*243. \n \nSHRI S. R. VIJAYAKUMAR:\n \nSHRI DEVAJIBHAI G. FATEPARA:\n       \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \na)\n \nthe objectives of the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY), the \npresent status of its implementation and the details of programmes drawn to impart \ntraining in various \ncategories under the scheme, State\n-\nwise;\n \n \nb)\n \nwhether the Government has given facilities such as travelling allowance, \nboarding and lodging cost to the trainees and if so, the details thereof and the criteria \nadopted by the Government in this regard;\n \n \nc)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to revise the targets under the PMKVY \nand if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;\n \n \nd)\n \nthe funds allocated/sanctioned/utilised under the scheme so far along with the \ntargets set and achievements made, year\n-\nwise, Sta\nte/UT\n-\nwise; and\n \n \ne)\n \nwhether various States have submitted proposals for setting up of institutes to \npromote PMKVY in their States and if so, the follow\n-\nup action taken thereon, State\n-\nwise?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \n \nSK\nILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY)\n \n \n \n(a) to (e)\n \n \n  \nA Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 243 TO BE ANSWERED ON 03.08.2016 ASKED BY \nSHRI S. R. VIJAYAKUMAR AND SHRI DEVAJIBHAI G. FATEPARA\n \nREGARDING \nFUNDS UNDER PMKVY\n \n \n \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) 2015\n-\n16 had a target to \ncover 24 lakh youth in the country. The scheme was implemented by National Skill \nDevelopment Corporatio\nn (NSDC) with the objective to enable a large number of \nIndian youth to take up industry\n-\nrelevant skill training that will help them in securing a \nbetter livelihood. Individuals with prior learning experience or skills were also \nassessed and certified unde\nr Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) category.\n \n \nThe total numbers to be trained under the scheme was 24 lakhs spread \nacross Fresh Training (14 lakhs) and Recognition of Prior Learning (10 lakhs). \nUnder \nthe scheme, a\ns on 28\nth\n \nJuly 2016, a total of 17.94 la\nkh candidates (under Fresh \nTraining) have been enrolled, 17.93 lakh have been trained and 11.9 lakh have been \ncertified (\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n).\n \n \n \nUnder the scheme, monetary reward was provided to trainees who are \nsuccessfully trained, assessed and certified in skill\n \ncourses run by affiliated training \nproviders. It also sought to significantly scale up skill training activities in the country \nand enable skill training to happen at a fast pace without compromising quality. \nInstitutional arrangements comprising of the N\national Skill Development Corporation \n(NSDC), Sector Skill Councils (SSCs), Assessing agencies and Training Partners \nwere put in place for implementation of the scheme.\n \n \nRecently, the Union Cabinet in its meeting on 13\nth\n \nJuly, 2016, has approved \nthe new ve\nrsion with modification and continuation of the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojana (PMKVY) as ‘Skill Development Component’ of umbrella PMKVY to \nskill one crore people over the four years (2016\n-\n2020) with an outlay of Rs.12,000 \ncrore. The Scheme will be c\nompletely aligned to the Common Norms as notified with \nthe approval of Cabinet on 15\nth\n \nJuly, 2015, and amended from time to time. \nAlso, the \nCommon Norms provide for incentives in the form of reimbursement of conveyance \ncosts to Special Groups which \nincludes Women, SC/ST and Physical Handicapped \ncandidates.  Further, the scheme also provides for boarding and lodging of \ncandidates from North East States, Left Wing Extremism affected districts, Persons \nwith Disabilities (PwD) and others in accordance wi\nth the Common Norms \nguidelines.\n \n \nAs PMKVY is a nation\n-\nwide scheme, NSDC does not capture State\n-\nwise and \nitem\n-\nwise expenditure incurred under the scheme. However, funds amounting to \nRs.935 crore (Rs.435 crore in 2015\n-\n16 and Rs.500 crore in 2016\n-\n17) have bee\nn \nallocated to NSDC for PMKVY, till 30\nth\n \nJune, 2016.\n \n \nUnder the PMKVY 2015\n-\n16 there was no State component for allocation of \nfunds to State Governments. However, under PMKVY 2016\n-\n20\n \nin order to address \nthe unique skill requirements of different States, fun\nds are being directly allocated to \nState Governments through a project based approach, with 25% of the total training \ntargets, both financial and physical.  The States are expected to finalize projects \nbased on critical gaps in skilling within their States\n \nin terms of sectors, job roles, \ngeography etc. and strictly follow Common Norms for arriving at the project cost. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nState\n \nTotal \nEnrolled\n \nTotal Trainings \nCompleted\n \nTotal \nCertified\n \nAndaman and Nicobar Islands\n \n194\n \n194\n \n19\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n124666\n \n124516\n \n91919\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n1017\n \n1017\n \n337\n \nAssam\n \n31224\n \n31194\n \n2180\n \nBihar\n \n89252\n \n89166\n \n51572\n \nChandigarh\n \n4851\n \n4851\n \n3709\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n36488\n \n36488\n \n23857\n \nDadra and Nagar Haveli\n \n258\n \n258\n \n159\n \nDaman and Diu\n \n230\n \n230\n \n177\n \nDelhi\n \n75194\n \n75171\n \n49782\n \nGoa\n \n499\n \n499\n \n278\n \nGujarat\n \n43324\n \n43324\n \n27095\n \nHaryana\n \n81612\n \n81474\n \n56217\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n22738\n \n22738\n \n16718\n \nJammu and Kashmir\n \n17807\n \n17807\n \n10985\n \nJharkhand\n \n26488\n \n26463\n \n18406\n \nKarnataka\n \n73523\n \n73523\n \n49247\n \nKerala\n \n14689\n \n14689\n \n10049\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n159139\n \n158974\n \n107151\n \nMaharashtra\n \n84432\n \n84312\n \n52984\n \nManipur\n \n1328\n \n1328\n \n807\n \nMeghalaya\n \n1661\n \n1661\n \n16\n \nMizoram\n \n1030\n \n1030\n \n5\n \nNagaland\n \n1271\n \n1271\n \n744\n \nOdisha\n \n56822\n \n56822\n \n34666\n \nPuducherry\n \n7070\n \n7070\n \n5924\n \nPunjab\n \n72405\n \n72350\n \n51064\n \nRajasthan\n \n112467\n \n112437\n \n81061\n \nSikkim\n \n886\n \n886\n \n379\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n151491\n \n151491\n \n107972\n \nTelangana\n \n98323\n \n98263\n \n73530\n \nTripura\n \n14018\n \n14018\n \n9582\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n256469\n \n256365\n \n175503\n \nUttarakhand\n \n13675\n \n13675\n \n9191\n \nWest Bengal\n \n117925\n \n117925\n \n76400\n \nTOTAL :\n \n1794466\n \n1793480\n \n1199685\n \n \n        \n***\n****\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_244": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n   \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.*244 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd AUGUST, 2016 \n \nREVIEW OF NUTP \n \nNo.*244             DR. KAMBHAMPATI HARIBABU: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state :  \n \n(a) whether the Government has reviewed/proposes to review the \nprogress made under the National Urban Transport Policy (NUTP), \nif so, the details and the outcome thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government  consid ers any change in  NUTP and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government had got  a study conducted in the year \n2012-13 for establishing serv ice level benchmarks for urban \ntransport; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and t he steps taken by the Government \nin this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU) \n  \n(a) to (d)  :  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n                                                ****** \n\n  \nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*244  FOR ANSWER ON 3 RD \nAUGUST, 2016 REGARDING  REVIEW OF NUTP . \n \n(a)  :   National Urban Transport Policy (NUTP) is a policy guideline \nwhich provides broad fram ework for development of urban transport.  \nSince, Urban Transport, intertwined with Urban Development, is a \nState subject, planning, executi on and development of urban \ntransport facilities are done by th e concerned State/Union Territory \n(UT).  Financial assist ance for projects of Urban transport like Buses, \nBus Rapid Transit System (BRTS) , Metro Rail Systems etc. and for \npreparation of Feasibility Study Repo rts, Detailed Project Reports and \nComprehensive Mobility Plans is pr ovided and reviewed from time to \ntime under different schemes. A brief on the progress made in Urban \nTransport projects and study reports are given in Annexure 1. \n(b):      No, Madam \n(c) & (d): Yes, Madam. Service Level Benchmarks (SLB) for Urban \nTransport were finalised and circulat ed to States/UTs in November, \n2009. The Government started a research study in the year 2012-13 in \n12 cities viz., Delhi, Jaipur, Jammu-Katra, Nanded, Patna, Vijayawada, \nKohima, Bhubaneswar, Ahmedabad, Surat, Mysore and Hubli–Dharwad \nwith an objective to develop operat ional guidelines and methodology \nfor undertaking benchmarking using the above SLB in two stages.\n  \nStage one of the stud y, comprising devel opment of operational \nguidelines and methodology of delineating urban area, data collection, \nstorage and analysis and stand ardising the procedure for undertaking \nbenchmarking, has been complet ed. Stage two, consisting of \nmonitoring the change in level of se rvice of urban transport in these \n12 cities, is in progress. A toolki t of standard procedure/process for \ncarrying out benchmarking of transport in urban areas will also be  \n\n \nprepared as part of study.  Further, establishing SLBs have been made \na mandatory requirement under revi sed Comprehensive Mobility Plan \ntoolkit issued in September,  2014 and Atal Mission for Rejuvenation \nand Urban Transformation (AMRUT) scheme launched in June-2015.  \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE- 1 \nANNEXURE- 1 REFERRED TO IN REPL Y OF PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *244 REGARDING “REVIEW OF NUTP” TO BE \nANSWERED ON 03.08.2016 ASKED BY DR. KAMBHAMPATI HARIBABU. \n \nA Brief on the progress made in Urban Transport Projects \n \n About 316 Km of Metro Rail   is under operation and more than 500 \nKm is under construction. This  includes Metro/Mono Rail systems \npromoted by State Governments and Private Bodies under their own \narrangements; \n Ministry of Urban Development (MoUD) has sanctioned 25,485 \nbuses and 154 ancillary infrastruct ure projects such as creation/ \nupgradation of Depots, termi nals & ITS facilities to 157 \ncities/cluster of cities. Funding under Atal Mi ssion for Rejuvenation \nand Urban Transformation (AMRUT) has been continued till \n31.03.2017 for 7,509 buses  and 25 ancillary infrastructure projects \n(sanctioned during the Transition Phase i.e. 2012-13 and 2013-14). \nAdditional Central Assistance ( ACA) amounting to Rs. 2740 crore \n(approx.) has been released. Mo re than 18,000 buses have been \nprocured so far;  \n MoUD has provided financial assistance to 11 cities for \nconstruction of about 504 Km Bus Rapid Transit System (BRTS).  \nAbout 222 km of BRTS is operatio nal and remaining about 282 km \nis under construction; \n Under the Urban Transpor t Planning & Capacity Building Scheme, \nthe Ministry has provided financial assistance up to 80% for \npreparation of CMP in 77 cities.  Further, financial assistance up to \n50% has been provided for preparat ion of DPR of 22 Metro Rail \nprojects of various cities. Vari ous other traffic & transportation \nstudies including research in tr ansport and training under Capacity \nBuilding have been funded under this scheme.   \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_245": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.245 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.08.2016 \n \nTRACK RENEWAL WORK \n \n*245.    SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \n             SHRI ADHALRAO PATIL SHIVAJIRAO:  \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total length of railway tracks in the country; \n \n(b) whether more than 5300 km length of the track is due for renewal and if \nso, the details of track-wise reasons for the backlog of renewal projects; \n \n(c) whether arrears of track renewal are accumulati ng year by year resulting \nin disproportionately high maintenance and reduced reliability of assets  \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Railways has prepared any target plan for track renewal \nduring the Twelfth Plan period and if so, the details thereof, zone-wise; and \n \n(e) the steps taken by the Railways to speed up the track renewal works to \nreduce the maintenance cost and increase the reliability of assets? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI SURESH PRABHAKAR PRABHU) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTON NO. 245 BY SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT  AND SHRI ADHALRAO \nPATIL SHIVAJIRAO TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.08.2016 \nREGARDING TRACK RENEWAL WORK. \n \n(a) The total railway track length as on 31.3.2015 is 1,17,996 Kilometers. \n \n(b) & (c): Track renewal is a continuous activity, which is to be undertaken as \nand when a stretch of track becomes due for renewal on age -cum-condition \nbasis. Track renewal are planned in advance every year and their execution is \nprioritized according to condition o f track and overall availability of funds \nensuring all the time that the track is in a sound condition for safe running of \ntrains In case, if any stretch of track is not renewed in time due to various \nreasons including scarcity of funds, suitable speed res trictions is imposed to \nensure safe running of trains. The sanctioned track renewal yet to be \ncompleted have reduced from 7258 km for the year 2008 -09 to 5300 km for the \nyear 2014-15. Further, it has reduced to 5100 km in 2015-16.  \n \n(d): Track renewal targ ets are fixed every year. The details of track renewal \ntargets during twelfth plan period, zone-wise are as below: \n Complete Track renewal Units (CTR Units) \nRailway 2012-13 2013-14* 2014-15* 2015-16 2016-17 \nCentral Railway 244 156 200 220 124 \nEastern Railway 207 195 183 200 108 \nEast Central Railway 200 149 171 150 105 \nEast Coast Railway 71 70 62 110 58 \nNorthern Railway 627 359 385 390 299 \nNorth Central Railway 257 203 130 150 94 \nNorth Eastern Railway 112 48 68 90 44 \nNortheast Frontier \nRailway \n76 53 27 50 21 \nNorth Western Railway 58 24 24 50 40 \nSouthern Railway 133 90 102 150 111 \nSouth Central Railway 285 197 175 190 67 \nSouth Eastern Railway 156 111 110 150 58 \nSouth East Central \nRailway \n84 71 102 120 98 \nSouth Western Railway 117 105 102 110 59 \nWestern Railway 194 143 165 190 121 \nWest Central Railway 179 126 194 180 93 \nTotal 3000 2100 2200 2500 1500 \nNote:- * denotes Revised Targets \n\n \n (e) Steps taken by Railways to speed up Track renewal , to reduce \nmaintenance cost and increase the reliability of assets are as under: \n(i) The track length sanctioned for renewal in 2016 -17 has been increased  \nfrom 1028 km in 2014-15 to 2950 km in year 2016-17. \n(ii) The progress of Track renewal has increased from 2424 km in 2014-15 to \n2794 km in 2015-16. \n(iii) Bio Toilets are being provided in trains to prevent corrosion of rails due \nto toilet droppings. All Trains running between Rameswaram and \nManmadurai in Southern Railway have been fitted with Bio Toilets. This \nsection has been declared Green Corridor. \n(iv) A proposal for creation of graded Rastriya Rail Sanraksha Kosh (RRSK) of \ntotal `119183 crores for safety related works to be operated over next \nfive years has been sent to Ministry of Finance for reconsideration, the \ndetails of which are as under.  \nItem of Work Total \nAnticipated \namount \nCivil Engineering Assets including Track Renewal, \nrehabilitation of Bridges and Isolation of Railway Line etc. \n` 44979 Cr. \nSafety Works at Level crossings including construction of \nROBs/RUBs. \n`  43444 Cr. \nS&T Department (Provision of Train Protection warning \nsystem, upgradation of Signalling system, etc.) \n`  10140 Cr. \nMechanical Engineering Department (Freight and Coach \ndesign and maintenance etc.) \n`  9236.55 Cr.  \nElectrical Engineering Department ( Replacement of \noveraged Traction Distribution System, audio/video \nrecording of Loco cabs, etc.) \n`  9495 Cr. \nHuman Resource Development which includes \nUpgradation of training institutes, running rooms, crew \nlobbies and setting up of safety village. \n`  1861.44 Cr \nTotal `  119183 Cr.  \n\n(v) Electronic monitoring of track geometry with Track Recording Cars \n(TRC) and Portable Oscillation Monitoring (OMS) Systems to detect \ntrack geometry defects to plan  maintenance timely \n(vi) Scheduled inspection of track by Railway Officials as per laid down \nfrequency \n(vii) Use of Technology like Track Management System to decide \nrationalized maintenance requirement and optimized inputs by \nproviding database and decision support system. \n(viii) Improved tools with  reduced weight have been standardized for \ntrackmen to help them in better maintenance of tracks. \n \nTrack renewal works are prioritized according to overall \navailability of funds ensuring all the time that track is in sound \ncondition for safe running of tra ins. As on 31.03.2016, Track \nRenewal and related works covering 2950 km have been \nsanctioned. In addition, 2950 km Track Renewal have also been \nsanctioned in 2016-17.  As per norms, Track Renewals works are to \nbe completed within two to three years of sanc tion. This, however, \nalso depends on budget availability. \n \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_246": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 246  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 3RD AUGUST, 2016 \n \nFUNDS UNDER MPLADS \n \n*246.   SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:  \n   SHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n     \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the funds allocated and released under the Member of \nParliament Local Area Development (MPLAD) Scheme \nduring the last three years and the current year;  \n(b)  the funds utilised and durable work created thereunder;  \n(c) whether there has been any delay in releasing the funds \nunder the said scheme and if so, the details thereof along \nwith the reasons therefor and the corrective steps taken \nthereon; and  \n(d) whether the Gov ernment has appointed any committee/or \nany mechanism exists for reviewing the implementation of \nMPLAD Scheme and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n[SHRI  D.V. SADANANDA GOWDA] \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred \nQuestion No. 246 for answer on 03.08.2016. \n \n(a) & (b): The annual entitlement of honourable Members of \nParliament (MPs) is Rs 5 c rore per annum , which is released in two \nequal instalments  of Rs 2.5 crore each  by Government of India \ndirectly to the District Authority of the Nodal District of the MP \nconcerned.  \nThe works are recommended by the honourable MPs and are \nsanctioned as well  as executed through District Authorities \nconcerned. The specific data is maintained at the District level and \nonly broad parameters are maintained by this Ministry. Based on \ninformation received from the District Authorities, the data for the \nyears 2013-14, 2014-15, 2015-16 and 2016 -17 (as on 30.06.2016) is \nas under:- \n \nYear Released by \nGOI  \n(Rs. in crore) \nWorks \ncompleted \n(No.) \nExpenditure \nincurred \n(Rs. in crore) \nUnspent \nbalance \n(Rs. in crore) \n2013-14 3937.00 115846 4380.24 4024.35 \n2014-15 3350.00 83095 2660.69 4857.51 \n2015-16 3502.00 91368 3628.01 4947.13 \n2016-17  \n(up to 30.06.2016) \n858.00 26328 997.50 4963.77 \n \nNote: (i) The figures are based on the latest monthly progress reports received \nfrom the Nodal District Authorities.  \n \n(ii) There is no incongruity in the figures since the funds are non -lapsable and \nunspent balances as well as interest are utilized in the subsequent year(s).  \n \n(iii) Unspent balance figures are net cumulative amount available with the Nodal \nDistricts as on 31.03.2014 for the year 2013 -14, 31.03.2015 for the year 2014 -15, \n31.03.2016 for the year 2015-16 and as on 30.06.2016 for the year 2016-17. \n \n(c): Release of funds under the MPLADS at the Central Government \nlevel as well as at the District level is governe d by the  provisions \ncontained in the Guidelines on MPLADS.   \n \nThe first installment of MPLADS funds is released subject to \nsubmission of provisional Utilisation Certificate (UC) of the previous \nyear for at least 80% of expenditure of the first instalment of the \nprevious year. The second installment is released on submission of \n\nMonthly Progress Report (MPR) reflecting unsanctioned balance of \nless than Rs.100 lakh and unspent balance of less than Rs.250 lakh \nwith the District Authority along with the Utilisa tion Certificate (UC) \nand Audit Certificate (AC) of the immediately concluded financial \nyear. As soon as the requisite documents and certifications are \nreceived, the funds are released by the Central Government.   \n \nThe District Authorities release funds to  the Implementing Agencies \nin accordance with the State Government’s rules/guidelines \napplicable for the purpose.  \n \nFunds under the MPLADS are non -lapsable, both at the end of the \nCentral Government and at the end of the District Authorities. \nMembers of Parliament can recommend works upto their full annual \nentitlement without linking with actual release of funds and Dis trict \nAuthorities are required to undertake immediate sanction and \nimplementation of the eligible works so recommended. \n \nGovernment through the Ministry of Statistics & Programme \nImplementation continuously emphasizes on expeditious utilisation \nof funds an d timely submission of required documents and \ncertifications for further release of funds. \n \n(d): Implementation of the Members of Parliament Local Area \nDevelopment Scheme (MPLADS) in the field is undertaken by the \nDistrict Authorities in accordance with th e State Government ’s \ntechnical, administrative and financial rules and in accordance with \nthe Guidelines on MPLADS.  \n \nThe Guidelines are modified from time to time in order to improve \nthe design and implementation of the Scheme. \n \nThe updated Guidelines on MPLADS as on June 2016 are available in \nthe public domain, including on the official website of Ministry of \nStatistics and Programme Implementation (www.mospi.nic.in). \n \nMonitoring mechanisms, wherein the roles of the Central \nGovernment, State Governments, District Authorities and \nImplementing Agencies are laid down, have been duly prescribed in \nthe Guidelines.  \n \n\nThe Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation regu larly \nreviews the implementation of the MPLADS through national -level \nreview meetings with State Government / District officers and visits \nto States / Districts. The Ministry also undertakes third party \nphysical monitoring of the MPLADS works in selected d istricts \nthrough independent agencies. In addition, the Comptroller and \nAuditor General (C & AG) of India conducts periodic Performance \nAudit of the MPLADS. \n \nWherever / whenever violations of Guidelines or irregularities in \nimplementation of the MPLAD Sche me come to notice in the \nMinistry, the concerned State Government / District Authority is \nrequested to take appropriate action, including penal and \ndepartmental action and recovery of MPLADS funds with interest. \n \nThe Ministry continuously emphasises on the qualitative, rule-bound \nand speedy implementation of the works under the Scheme.  \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_247": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n    MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS  \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 247 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.08.2016 \n \nRECOMMENDATIONS OF RAILWAY COMMITTEES \n \n�������������������������������������������������������� \n   SHRI P.C. MOHAN: \n               \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS   be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has constituted any committee(s) \nto review the functioning of various wings of the Indian \nRailways, if so, the details thereof; \n \n  (b) whether the Railways has received the reports from these \ncommittees and if so, the major recommendations made \n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n \n (c)  whether the Railways has accepted these \nrecommendations and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n  (d) the other steps taken to implement the recommendations \nreceived from these committees?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI SURESH PRABHAKAR PRABHU) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 247 BY SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS AND \nSHRI P.C. MOHAN TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.08.2016 \nREGARDING RECOMMENDATIONS OF RAILWAY COMMITTEES. \n \n(a)�: In the long history of Indian Railways,  a large number of \nCommittees have been set up from time to time to examine \nvarious issues relating to the Railway working.  During the last \ntwo years, the following important Committees have been set \nup:- \n(i)�Committee under the Chairmanship of Dr. Bibek Debr oy set up in \nSeptember, 2014; \n(ii) High Level Committee to identify factors, issues and avenues for \nimproving financial health of Railways headed by Shri D.K.Mittal, \nset up in  December, 2014; \n(iii)One-man Committee headed by Dr. E.Sreedharan set up in \nNovember, 2014 to suggest proper system and procedures to \nensure proper accountability and transparency at the General \nManagers and other functionaries level for taking all Commercial \ndecisions, including that of tendering.  \n(b)�: Yes, Madam.  The major recommendations made by the \nCommittees are as under :- \n(i)� Dr. ������������ �������������� ������������������������ ������������ ��������������������������������\npertain to Organizational restructuring, re -organisation/ \nrationalization of multiple services/cadres, delegation of power, \naccounting reform, regulato ry authority, phase -out of rail budget, \nseparation of operations and infrastructure etc. Many of the \nrecommendations of this Committee were already part of the \nRailway Budget Speech 2015-16. \n\n�������� \n \n-2- \n(ii) D.K.Mittal Committee suggested ways and means to \nimprove   the   financial   health   of   Indian Railways.  It has \nsuggested short -term, medium term, long -term measures for \nimprovement in the areas of goods, passenger tariff revenues.  It \nhas also suggested measures that can be taken for long term \nresource mobilization and to improve efficiency and productivity \nof Indian Railways.  \n(iii) ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������- \n���� Decentralisation of purchase of Diesel/Petroleum Oil \nLubricant(POL), Rails, Sleeper, Track machines, Locomotives , \nTrain Sets, Coaches and Wagons to either Zonal Railways or \nProduction Units or Central Organization for Modernization of \nWorkshops.  \n����Delegation of full powers to General Managers for sanction of \ndetailed Estimates, Tenders and Re -appropriation of Funds a nd \nsubstantial enhancement of powers regarding Material \nmodification, rewards/honorariums and other miscellaneous \nmatters. \n \n����Research Designs & Standards Organisation (RDSO)  should \nhave no role in vendor approval and material inspection \nactivities. These activities should be performed by Zonal \nRailways/Production Units. \n����Complete transfer of catering to India n Railways Catering and \nTourism Corporation (IRCTC). \n \n����������- \n\n \n \n-3- \n(c) & (d):  Several recommendations of the Committees have \nbeen accepted for implementation. Many of the recommendations \nsuch as accounting reforms, delegation of powers and resource  \nmaximisation etc. also pertain to initiatives already undertaken by \nthe Ministry. A Committee-wise summary of the recommendations is \nas under:                                                                                        \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of the  \nCommittee  \nTotal No. of \nrecommenda\n-tions made \nNo. of  \nrecommendations \nacted upon/ \naccepted \nNo. of   \nRecommenda- \ntions  \npartially  \naccepted \n1 Bibek \nDebroy \nCommittee \n40 8 2 \n2 D.K. Mittal \nCommittee \n \n239 69 17 \n3 E. \nSreedharan \nCommittee \n167 67 45 \n \n***\n\n�",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_248": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *248 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, AUGUST 3, 2016 \n \nHIGH PERFORMANCE COMPUTING \n \n*248.  SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n  \nWill the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state:  \n(a)  the progress made in the strategic programme of the High \nPerformance Computing (HPC) projects so far;  \n(b)  the expenditure incurred and the objectives achieved there  \nfrom; \n(c)  whether the desired progress has not been made due to funds \nconstraints a nd if so, the steps taken by the Ministry in \nconsultation with the Ministry of Finance to increase the \nbudgetary allocation for the project; and \n(d)  the present budgetary allocation for the project and the details of \nthe expansion plans of the project? \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER FOR MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND  \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \n (DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n(a)-(d)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA IN REPLY (a) to (d) TO \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *248 REGARDING “HIGH PERFORMANCE \nCOMPUTING” TO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, AUGUST 3, 2016 \n \n(a)   The National Supercomputing Mission (NSM) was approved by Cabinet \nCommittee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) in March 2015 with an outlay of \nRs 4500 Cr (Department of Science and Technology (DST) Rs 2740 Cr + \nDepartment of Electronics and Information Technology (DeitY) Rs 1760 \nCr) spread over a period of seven years. The main objective of the \nmission was creating supercomputing infrastructure of various \ncapacities, developing applications of national importance and \ncapacity building though human resources development. The mission is \nbeing jointly implemented by Centre for Development of Advanced \nComputing (C-DAC) and Indian Institute of Science (IISc) Banglore. \n \nNSM Technical Advisory Committee (NSM -TAC) was constituted under \nthe chairmanship of Dr. V. K. Saraswat , Member, Niti Aayog to provide \ndue guidance for implementation. Constitution of four Expert Groups (i) \nInfrastructure; (ii) Research and Development (R&D); (iii) Application \nDevelopment; and (iv) Human Resources Development were setup to \nprovide guidance for executing the mission.  At present technology \npartners are being identified for NSM through the process of \nExpression of Interest (EOI). \n \nNSM Expert Group on Application Development has identified the \nfollowing preliminary applications for taking up R&D projects.  \n \n Drug Discovery platform for neglected diseases.   \n Predictive and personalized medicine. \n A package of analysis and design of engineering products and \nprocesses (including SME, MSME). \n Design of molecules and materials for societal benefits. \n Integrated weather and disaster predictions and seismic \nimaging.  \n Urban well-being. \n Biomedical and clinical data warehouse with multipurpose tool \nfor mining the integrated resources. \n Biodiversity mapping through remote sensing. \n A collection of tools and metho ds for NSM including cyber \nsecurity. \n E-Teacher: Engineering and Computations. \n Astronomy, Astrophysics and high energy physics. \n Computational Chemistry. \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nIn addition, a separate programme “Implementation of HPC system as \napproved by CCEA on 6 th Feb 2014 for implementation during the 12 th \nplan with a total outlay of Rs 567.16 crores for augmenting the High \nPerformance Computing  (HPC) infrastructure in Ministry of Earth \nSciences towards the development of the state -of-art dynamical \nmodels for improving the weather and climate prediction. Accordingly, \ntwo HPC systems “Aaditiya” with a peak computing power of 790 \nTeraflops and \"Bhas kara\" with a peak computing power of 350 Tera  \nFlops have been installed and commissioned at Indian Institute of \nTropical Meteorology (IITM), Pune and National Centre of Medium \nRange Weather Forecasting (NCMRWF), NOIDA respectively. \n \n(b)  Release of first installment of Grant in Aid of Rs 50.0 Crore and Rs 70.0 \nCrore was made by DeitY and DST to C -DAC and IISc respectively \ntowards launching preparatory activities. \n \nIn MoES, the expenditure incurred so far is Rs. 206.95 crore .  The \npresent HPC facility is being used for assimilating all type of data \navailable from various observing platforms and running high resolution \nweather and climate models for producing accurate weather forecast \nin short, medium, long range and climate s cale.  The HPC is also used \nfor the development of very high resolution dynamical models with high \ncomplexity and coupled ocean atmosphere models at monthly, \nseasonal and climate time scale.  \n \n(c)  No Madam. \n \n(d)  Budgetary allocation for 2015 -16 was 174 c rore and for 2016 -17 is \n812.50 crore. \n \nExperts groups on infrastructure is providing directions for entry level, \nmid range and large HPC systems setup in research institutes with \nvarying capacities and capabilities. The focus is to build systems in \nIndia as part of Make in India approach in a phased manner.  \n \n \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n  ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_249": "������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�\n��� ������ �\n�� �������������������� ��� �\n��������������������� �� � �� ���� ������ �������� �� � ������ ������� �\n�\n�������������� �\n�\n��� �� � ��������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������� ���\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� � ��������� ���� ������ ������ ��� �����\n����������� ������������������������������������������ ������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������� ��������������������������������������\n���� �������� �\n�\n����������������������������������� �\n�\n���� ���� ������ ������ ��� �������� �������� ���� ���� ������� ������ ������� ������\n����� ��� ������������������������������������������ ���������������������\n���������������� �\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������������� �\n������ ��������������������� ��\n�\n����� ���������� �\n����������� ��������������� �\n����� ��� ������ ��� �������� ������� ������������ � ����� ������� �� ������ ����\n���������� ������������� ������� �������������� ���� ��������� ��������� ��� ����\n������� ���� ���� ������ � ������� � ����� ���� ��������� ��� �������� ��� ��������\n���������� �������� ��� ���� ��������� ��������� ���������� ����������\n����������� �\n��������������� �",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_25": "भारत सरकार  \nरसायन और उर्वरक मंत्राऱय \nरसायन और पेट्रोरसायन वर्भाग \nऱोक सभा \nतारांककत प्रश्न संख्या - 25 \nददनांक 19 जुऱाई, 2015 को उत्तर ददए जाने क े  लऱए \n.....  \nइथेनॉऱ का उपयोग   \n25.  डॉ. स् र्ामस साषीजसजस मराराज: \n \n क्मा रसायन और उर्वरक भंत्री मह फताने की कृऩा कयेंगे कक:  \n \n (क) गत तीन वर्षों क े  दौयान यसामन उद्मोग भें उऩमोग भें राए गए इथेनॉर की भात्रा ककतनी है;   \n(ख)  क् मा भंत्रारम ने ऩेरोलोर भें इथेनॉर को लभराए नाने को िवनवामब फनाए नाने का यसामन उद्मोग \nऩय वास् तविवक ूपऩ से ऩ़नने वारे ्रभबाव का िध् ममन ककमा है, मदद हां, तो तत् संफंधी ‍ मौया क् मा \nहै;  \n (ग) क् मा भंत्रारम ने देम भें इथेनॉर लभरित त ऩेरोलोर क े  िवनवामब उऩमोग क े  संफंध भें िऩनी \n आऩत्त् तमां ‍ मक् त की हं; औय  \n (घ) मदद हां, तो इसक े  क् मा कायह हं ?  \n \nउत्तर \n \nरसायन और उर्वरक एर्ं संसदीय कायव मंत्रस (श्रस अनन् त कुमार) \n \n(क) :   उद्मोग के िनुभानों के िनुसाय, गत तीन वर्षों के दौयान यसामन उद्मोग भें ्रभमोग हुए इथेनॉर \nकी भात्रा वनम्नानुसाय है : \nवर्षब 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15  \nभात्रा (कयो़न रीटय भें) 84 84 87 \n \n(ख) : विवबाग ने ऩेरोलोर भें  इथेनॉर क े  लभ ह को िवनवामब फनाए नाने का यसामन उद्मोग ऩय ऩ़नने \nवारे संबाविवत ्रभबाव का कोई औऩचारयक िध्ममन नहीं ककमा है ।  \n \n(ग) एवं (घ) : इथेनॉर एक भहत् वऩूहब यसामन है त्नसका उऩमोग कई ्रभभुख वाणहत्यमक यसामनों नैसे \nएलसदटक एलसड,  इथाइर एसीटेट,  भोनो  इथाइरीन,  ग्राइकोर  (एभईनी)-ऩॉलरएस् टय  (ऩीईटी)  येत्नन,  \n\n- 2 - \n \n \nऩॉलरएस्टय पाइफय/मानब आदद के  विववनभाबह भें ककमा नाता है । इथेनॉर का उऩमोग फल् क ड्रग्स, और्षरितधमों \nक े  विववनभाबह भें साल्वेंट औय मुवििकयह एनेंट तथा ्रभमोगमारा भें यी-एनेंट क े  ूपऩ भें ककमा नाता है । \nइथेनॉर, यसामन उद्मोग क े  विवकास औय भेक इन इंडडमा कामबक्रभ क े  रक्ष्मों को ्रभाप्त कयने क े लरए  \nित्मन्त भहत्वऩूहब है । इसक े िवतरयक्त, ईफीऩी कामबक्रभ का सपरताऩूवबक कक्रमान्वमन बी इथेनॉर की \nऩमाबप्त उऩर‍धता ऩय वनबबय कयता है ।   \n \n \n विवलबन्न ऺेत्रों क े  लरए इथेनॉर की उऩर‍धता सयर फनाने क े उद़देश् म से विवबाग ने इथेनॉर  क े  \nिरितधक उत्ऩादन क े  लरए कई उऩामों की लसपारयम की है त्ननभें  फेहतय ऩिवतमां नैसे भृदा स्वास््म देख-\nयेख, वंमानुगत ऩादऩ साभग्री एवं पदटबगेमन के साथ सूक्ष्भ लसंचाई आदद को िऩनाकय गन्ने क े  उत्ऩादन \nको फढ़ाना मालभर है ।   \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_26": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 26 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nCOMPUTERISATION OF PDS \n \n*26.  SHRI G. HARI: \nSHRI B. SRIRAMULU: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION  miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has  undertaken a programme to fully  \ncomputerize the Public Distribution System (PDS), link it with Aadhaar \nand clean up the family card data base to control the leakages; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the time -frame fixed for its \ncompletion indicating the names of the States where it  is fu lly \nimplemented as on date; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has  received any request from some \nStates for extension of the said time-frame in view of the problems faced \nin its implementation; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government \nthereto? \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 26* DUE FOR ANSWER ON 19.07.2016 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \n(a) Under 12th Five Year Plan (2012 -17), Government has approved a plan scheme on \n‘End-to-end Computerization of TPDS Operations’ which comprises of activities namely, \ndigitization of ration cards/benefici ary and other databases,  online allocation,  \ncomputerization of supply -chain management, setting up of transparency portals and \ngrievance redressal mechanisms etc. Besides above, States/UTs have been also requested to \nseed the Aadhaar numbers in Ration Card database and install devices for biometric \nauthentication at Fair Price Shops, to control leakages.  \n(b)  Being a plan scheme, the project period is upto 31.3.2017 and States/UTs have been \nasked to complete the project within the stipulated time. A statem ent showing the present \nstatus of implementation of various activities of the scheme and seeding of Aadhaar in \nbeneficiaries’ data base and installation of E-Point of Sale devices, (State-wise) is annexed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         \n(c) & (d)   All States are at different stages of implementation of ‘End -to-end \nComputerization of TPDS Operations’ scheme, as can be seen from the progress at \nannexure. \n***  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEX \nANNEXURE  REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 26* DUE FOR ANSWER ON 19.07.2016 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \nStatus of End-to-End Computerization of TPDS Operations \n(as on 12.07.2016) \nSl. States/UTs \nDigitization    \nof Ration \nCards(RCs) \nAadhaar \nSeeding in RCs \nOnline \nAllocation of \nFoodgrains \nComputerization of \nSupply-chain \nManagement \nTransparency \nPortal \nOnline \nGrievance \nRedressal \nToll Free \nHelpline \nNumbers \n% installation of e-\nPoSat FPS  \n1 Andaman & Nicobar  100% 32% Partial  - Yes Yes Yes 1% \n2 Andhra Pradesh  100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 100% \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 100% 1.57% - - Yes - Yes - \n4 Assam 100% - Implemented - Yes Yes Yes - \n5 Bihar 100% 0.06% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes - \n6 Chandigarh 100% 95% NA NA Yes Yes Yes NA \n7 Chhattisgarh 100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 95% \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 100% 74% - - Yes Yes Yes - \n9 Daman & Diu 100% 90% Implemented Partial Yes Yes Yes 100% \n10 Delhi 100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 1% \n11 Goa 100% 95% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes - \n12 Gujarat 100% 77% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 99% \n13 Haryana 100% 88% Implemented - Yes Yes Yes - \n14 Himachal Pradesh 100% 95% - - Yes Yes Yes - \n15 Jammu and Kashmir 100% 62% Partial - Yes - Yes - \n16 Jharkhand 100% 92% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 9% \n17 Karnataka 100% 88% Implemented Implemented Yes - Yes 19% \n18 Kerala 100% 95% - - Yes Yes Yes - \n19 Lakshadweep 100% 97% - NA Yes Yes Yes - \n20 Madhya Pradesh 100% 67% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes 100% \n21 Maharashtra 100% 87% Implemented - Yes Yes Yes - \n22 Manipur 100% 0.37% Partial - Yes Yes Yes - \n23 Meghalaya 100% - - - Yes Yes Yes - \n24 Mizoram 100% 0.39% - - Yes Yes Yes - \n25 Nagaland 100% 0.41% - - Yes Yes Yes - \n26 Odisha 100% 66% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes - \n27 Puducherry  100% 95% NA NA Yes Yes Yes NA \n28 Punjab